% Command-Line Interface
% Chapter 6 of the literate BitTorrent client

\section{Command-Line Interface}

The command-line interface is the user's first point of contact with the
BitTorrent client. A well-designed CLI makes the tool intuitive to use while
providing flexibility through options. We use the [[clap]] crate with its
derive macros to define our argument structure declaratively, gaining automatic
help text generation, validation, and shell completion support.

\subsection{CLI Design Principles}

Our CLI follows common conventions for Unix command-line tools:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Required positional argument}: The torrent file path is the
  primary input and is required. Placing it as a positional argument makes
  the common case ([[literate-bittorrent file.torrent]]) simple.

\item \textbf{Sensible defaults}: Optional arguments have reasonable defaults
  that work for most use cases. The output directory defaults to the current
  directory, and the port defaults to 6881 (the traditional BitTorrent port).

\item \textbf{Short and long options}: Both forms are provided for frequently
  used options ([[{-}o]] and [[{-}{-}output]], [[{-}v]] and [[{-}{-}verbose]]).

\item \textbf{Helpful error messages}: Invalid input produces clear messages
  explaining what went wrong and how to fix it.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The Args Structure}

We define the CLI arguments using [[clap]]'s derive macros. Each field becomes
either a positional argument (no [[long]] or [[short]] attribute) or an option.
The [[Parser]] derive macro generates the parsing code, while [[#[command]]]
and [[#[arg]]] attributes configure behavior and documentation.

<<cli args>>=
use clap::Parser;
use std::path::PathBuf;

/// A pedagogical BitTorrent client implemented as a literate program.
///
/// Downloads files from the BitTorrent network using a torrent file or
/// magnet link. This client prioritizes clarity and educational value
/// over performance optimizations.
#[derive(Parser, Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
#[command(name = "literate-bittorrent")]
#[command(author, version, about, long_about = None)]
pub struct Args {
    /// Path to the .torrent file to download
    ///
    /// The torrent file contains metadata about the download including
    /// file names, sizes, piece hashes, and tracker URLs.
    #[arg(value_name = "TORRENT")]
    pub torrent: PathBuf,

    /// Output directory for downloaded files
    ///
    /// Files will be saved to this directory, creating it if necessary.
    /// Defaults to the current working directory.
    #[arg(short, long, value_name = "DIR", default_value = ".")]
    pub output: PathBuf,

    /// Listen port for incoming peer connections
    ///
    /// The client will listen on this port for connections from other
    /// peers. The default port 6881 is the traditional BitTorrent port.
    /// Ports 6881-6889 are commonly used.
    #[arg(short, long, default_value_t = 6881)]
    pub port: u16,

    /// Enable verbose (DEBUG level) logging
    ///
    /// When enabled, the client outputs detailed logging information
    /// useful for debugging connection issues, protocol exchanges,
    /// and download progress.
    #[arg(short, long)]
    pub verbose: bool,
}
@

The [[Args]] structure captures all command-line configuration. Let's examine
each field:

\begin{description}
\item[torrent] A required positional argument for the torrent file path.
  The [[value\_name = "TORRENT"]] makes the help text clearer by showing
  [[<TORRENT>]] rather than [[<TORRENT\_PATH>]].

\item[output] The output directory with [[{-}o]] short form and [[{-}{-}output]]
  long form. The [[default\_value = "."]] means files are saved in the current
  directory if not specified.

\item[port] The listen port for peer connections. We use [[default\_value\_t]]
  (note the [[\_t]] suffix) because this is a typed default rather than a
  string that needs parsing.

\item[verbose] A boolean flag that enables DEBUG-level logging. Flags don't
  take values; their presence sets the field to [[true]].
\end{description}

\subsection{Argument Parsing}

The [[parse]] method provided by the [[Parser]] trait handles argument parsing.
We wrap this in a custom function to make testing easier and to provide a
clean interface for the main program.

<<cli parse>>=
impl Args {
    /// Parse command-line arguments from the process arguments.
    ///
    /// This is the primary entry point for CLI parsing. It reads from
    /// `std::env::args()` and exits the process with a helpful error
    /// message if parsing fails.
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```no_run
    /// use literate_bittorrent::cli::Args;
    ///
    /// let args = Args::parse_args();
    /// println!("Downloading: {:?}", args.torrent);
    /// ```
    pub fn parse_args() -> Self {
        Args::parse()
    }

    /// Parse arguments from an iterator of strings.
    ///
    /// This is useful for testing or when embedding the CLI in another
    /// program. The first element should be the program name (it will
    /// be used in error messages).
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::cli::Args;
    /// use std::path::PathBuf;
    ///
    /// let args = Args::try_parse_from([
    ///     "literate-bittorrent",
    ///     "test.torrent",
    ///     "-o", "/tmp/downloads",
    /// ]).unwrap();
    ///
    /// assert_eq!(args.torrent, PathBuf::from("test.torrent"));
    /// assert_eq!(args.output, PathBuf::from("/tmp/downloads"));
    /// ```
    pub fn try_parse_from<I, T>(iter: I) -> Result<Self, clap::Error>
    where
        I: IntoIterator<Item = T>,
        T: Into<std::ffi::OsString> + Clone,
    {
        <Args as Parser>::try_parse_from(iter)
    }
}
@

We provide two parsing methods:

\begin{itemize}
\item [[parse\_args]] reads from the actual command line and exits on error.
  This is what [[main]] will call.

\item [[try\_parse\_from]] accepts an iterator of strings, allowing tests to
  provide synthetic arguments. It returns a [[Result]] instead of exiting,
  making it suitable for testing error cases.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Module Structure}

The CLI module is intentionally simple, exporting just the [[Args]] structure
and its methods. The main program will use [[Args::parse\_args()]] to get
configuration, then pass the relevant fields to the download logic.

<<cli.rs>>=
// GENERATED CODE - DO NOT EDIT
// Source: nw/06-cli.nw
// Regenerate with: just tangle

//! Command-line interface for the BitTorrent client.
//!
//! This module defines the CLI using clap's derive macros, providing
//! automatic argument parsing, validation, and help text generation.
//!
//! # Usage
//!
//! ```no_run
//! use literate_bittorrent::cli::Args;
//!
//! let args = Args::parse_args();
//! println!("Torrent: {:?}", args.torrent);
//! println!("Output: {:?}", args.output);
//! println!("Port: {}", args.port);
//! println!("Verbose: {}", args.verbose);
//! ```

<<cli args>>

<<cli parse>>

<<cli tests>>
@

\subsection{Testing the CLI}

Testing command-line parsing requires care because we want to verify both
successful parsing and proper error handling. We use [[try\_parse\_from]]
to test with synthetic arguments, avoiding the need to manipulate process
arguments.

<<cli tests>>=
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn parse_required_torrent_path() {
        // AC #1: CLI accepts required positional argument for torrent file path
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "ubuntu.torrent",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert_eq!(args.torrent, PathBuf::from("ubuntu.torrent"));
    }

    #[test]
    fn parse_torrent_path_with_spaces() {
        // Paths with spaces should work when properly quoted
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "my downloads/ubuntu 22.04.torrent",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert_eq!(
            args.torrent,
            PathBuf::from("my downloads/ubuntu 22.04.torrent")
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn parse_absolute_torrent_path() {
        // Absolute paths should work
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "/home/user/torrents/file.torrent",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert_eq!(
            args.torrent,
            PathBuf::from("/home/user/torrents/file.torrent")
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn parse_output_directory_short() {
        // AC #2: CLI accepts -o/--output option for output directory
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
            "-o", "/tmp/downloads",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert_eq!(args.output, PathBuf::from("/tmp/downloads"));
    }

    #[test]
    fn parse_output_directory_long() {
        // AC #2: --output long form
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
            "--output", "/tmp/downloads",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert_eq!(args.output, PathBuf::from("/tmp/downloads"));
    }

    #[test]
    fn output_defaults_to_current_dir() {
        // AC #2: output defaults to current directory
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert_eq!(args.output, PathBuf::from("."));
    }

    #[test]
    fn parse_port_short() {
        // AC #3: CLI accepts -p/--port option for listen port
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
            "-p", "6889",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert_eq!(args.port, 6889);
    }

    #[test]
    fn parse_port_long() {
        // AC #3: --port long form
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
            "--port", "12345",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert_eq!(args.port, 12345);
    }

    #[test]
    fn port_defaults_to_6881() {
        // AC #3: port defaults to 6881
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert_eq!(args.port, 6881);
    }

    #[test]
    fn parse_verbose_short() {
        // AC #4: CLI accepts -v/--verbose flag for DEBUG logging
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
            "-v",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert!(args.verbose);
    }

    #[test]
    fn parse_verbose_long() {
        // AC #4: --verbose long form
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
            "--verbose",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert!(args.verbose);
    }

    #[test]
    fn verbose_defaults_to_false() {
        // AC #4: verbose defaults to false
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert!(!args.verbose);
    }

    #[test]
    fn parse_all_options_combined() {
        // All options can be used together
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "downloads/ubuntu.torrent",
            "-o", "/media/storage",
            "-p", "6999",
            "-v",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert_eq!(args.torrent, PathBuf::from("downloads/ubuntu.torrent"));
        assert_eq!(args.output, PathBuf::from("/media/storage"));
        assert_eq!(args.port, 6999);
        assert!(args.verbose);
    }

    #[test]
    fn parse_options_in_any_order() {
        // Options can appear before or after the positional argument
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "-v",
            "-o", "/tmp",
            "file.torrent",
            "-p", "7000",
        ]).unwrap();

        assert_eq!(args.torrent, PathBuf::from("file.torrent"));
        assert_eq!(args.output, PathBuf::from("/tmp"));
        assert_eq!(args.port, 7000);
        assert!(args.verbose);
    }

    #[test]
    fn help_output_contains_usage() {
        // AC #5: CLI generates --help output with usage examples
        let err = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "--help",
        ]).unwrap_err();

        let help_text = err.to_string();

        // Help should contain program name
        assert!(
            help_text.contains("literate-bittorrent"),
            "Help should contain program name: {}",
            help_text
        );

        // Help should mention the torrent argument
        assert!(
            help_text.contains("TORRENT") || help_text.contains("torrent"),
            "Help should mention torrent argument: {}",
            help_text
        );

        // Help should mention output option
        assert!(
            help_text.contains("output") || help_text.contains("-o"),
            "Help should mention output option: {}",
            help_text
        );

        // Help should mention port option
        assert!(
            help_text.contains("port") || help_text.contains("-p"),
            "Help should mention port option: {}",
            help_text
        );

        // Help should mention verbose option
        assert!(
            help_text.contains("verbose") || help_text.contains("-v"),
            "Help should mention verbose option: {}",
            help_text
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn missing_torrent_produces_helpful_error() {
        // AC #6: Invalid arguments produce helpful error messages
        let err = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
        ]).unwrap_err();

        let error_text = err.to_string();

        // Error should mention the missing argument
        assert!(
            error_text.contains("TORRENT") ||
            error_text.contains("required") ||
            error_text.contains("torrent"),
            "Error should mention missing TORRENT: {}",
            error_text
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn invalid_port_produces_helpful_error() {
        // AC #6: Invalid port value produces helpful error
        let err = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
            "-p", "not-a-number",
        ]).unwrap_err();

        let error_text = err.to_string();

        // Error should indicate the value is invalid
        assert!(
            error_text.contains("invalid") ||
            error_text.contains("Invalid") ||
            error_text.contains("not-a-number"),
            "Error should mention invalid port value: {}",
            error_text
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn port_overflow_produces_error() {
        // Port number exceeding u16 max should fail
        let err = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
            "-p", "99999",
        ]).unwrap_err();

        let error_text = err.to_string();
        assert!(
            error_text.contains("invalid") ||
            error_text.contains("Invalid") ||
            error_text.contains("99999"),
            "Error should mention invalid port: {}",
            error_text
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn unknown_option_produces_error() {
        // Unknown options should produce an error
        let err = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
            "--unknown-option",
        ]).unwrap_err();

        let error_text = err.to_string();
        assert!(
            error_text.contains("unexpected") ||
            error_text.contains("unknown") ||
            error_text.contains("--unknown-option"),
            "Error should mention unknown option: {}",
            error_text
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn args_is_clone() {
        // Args should be Clone for flexibility
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
        ]).unwrap();

        let cloned = args.clone();
        assert_eq!(args, cloned);
    }

    #[test]
    fn args_is_debug() {
        // Args should be Debug for logging
        let args = Args::try_parse_from([
            "literate-bittorrent",
            "file.torrent",
        ]).unwrap();

        let debug_str = format!("{:?}", args);
        assert!(debug_str.contains("Args"));
        assert!(debug_str.contains("file.torrent"));
    }
}
@

The tests cover each acceptance criterion:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{AC \#1}: Required torrent path parsing
\item \textbf{AC \#2}: Output directory option with default
\item \textbf{AC \#3}: Port option with default
\item \textbf{AC \#4}: Verbose flag
\item \textbf{AC \#5}: Help output generation
\item \textbf{AC \#6}: Error messages for invalid input
\end{itemize}

We also test edge cases like paths with spaces, options in different orders,
and the [[Clone]] and [[Debug]] trait implementations that make the type
convenient to use throughout the application.
